00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:01.429 align:start position:0%
 
let's talk to one of those republican  senators we've got josh hawley of  missouri joining us now from washington  senator hawley good morning to you  i want to get into a policy matter the  direct payments that you're pushing for  but before that  let's touch on two matters of politics  first mitch mcconnell most senior uh  republican in the senate  now congratulating president-elect biden  and kamala harris  do you join him in that congratulations  you know tony i'm not there yet i think  this process will end on january the 6th  which is when congress has to certify  the results of the electoral college  obviously the electoral college is very  significant  their vote very significant earlier but  the certification happens on january the  6th and  until then i think president trump  certainly has every right to  pursue the legal remedies he wants to  pursue and to be heard but  january 6th i think is the key date the  the legal runway is very short at this  point  january 6 is typically a formality  congress tabulates the votes to come in  from the states  mitch mcconnell is reportedly asking  senators like yourself  not to object to that formal count are  you considering objecting in the senate  well i'm studying what's been done in  the past tony and after the 2004  election and 2016  democrats raised concerns both in the  house and the senate this is really the  only forum that the constitution of the  laws give to  senators and to congress people the  opportunity to raise concerns about the  election so i'm reading what's been done  in the past  we've got a hearing today in the united  states senate on the election  uh so we'll see so you're well this  could come to a full vote then  both houses of congress on january 6  very interesting  in the meantime though many americans  are in need and there's a big discussion  on capitol hill about how to get them  relief  you and senator bernie sanders are  working together to push  the idea of direct payments checks  twelve hundred dollars  for individuals five hundred dollars for  children is that part of the latest bill  that was discussed last night among  leaders  i don't know the answer that yet tony  but i certainly hope it is because the  most  important thing that congress can do now  is to get assistance into the hands of  working families and working people  these are the folks who should be first  in line for coveted relief not last  they shouldn't be an afterthought  working people have borne the brunt of  this pandemic  and they need direct support and i'm  willing to do everything i can  everything in my power to make sure that  they get it  so the pushback from members of your own  party as well as democrats  is that the current bill the 748  billion dollar bill already includes  targeted payments relief for rent for  hunger  for joblessness and what you're talking  about is expensive and goes to everybody  as opposed to just the particular people  in need what's your response to that  well first of all working people are in  need and when you see the the people the  lines of folks lining up for food  support  uh trying to get help paying their rent  making their car payments i just talked  to a young mother in st louis with two  boys who said that she can either keep  making her car payments  uh or else she can do something for her  kids for christmas she can't do she  can't do all of it and this is this is  this story is being repeated over and  over  and over the need for food for instance  is through the roof in my state demand  for food up 50  in food banks here's the point tony  working people are in need  we're not talking about giving checks to  everybody for heaven's sakes we're  talking about working families we're  talking about those who have borne the  brunt of the pandemic and the quickest  way  to get them help and relief is to get  them direct support is to give them a  check like we did in march  so then here's the key question on the  principle that something is better than  nothing  are you willing to support because we  need everybody's support there in  congress for us to go forward  are you willing to support the 748  billion dollar bill they provide some  relief  if it doesn't include the checks that  you're talking about no i've made very  clear that unless there is direct  support for working americans i'm not  going to support covert relief why would  the government tony give hundreds of  billions of dollars  to big business or to government or to  other entities and not  give it to working families who need it  the most that makes  no sense whatsoever we need to target it  to the people who need it  the most and that's working families  quickly if you could center because we  have to go so you're prepared to hold  congress back from their holiday break  unless you get this direct vote on  payments every option is on the table  tony the working people of this country  need somebody to stand up and say they  need relief and that's what i'm going to  do  all right as you've said it's going to  be an interesting week senator hawley  thank you very much  thank you

